= Loss aversion =

== Overview ==
'''Loss aversion''' is the hypothesis that, relative to a reference point, losses can have greater psychological impact than commensurate gains.

== Prospect theory ==
Loss aversion is associated with [[Prospect theory]], where outcomes are evaluated as gains or losses relative to a reference point rather than solely as final wealth.

== Scope and debate ==
The magnitude and generality of loss aversion depend on task, stakes, endowment, experience, and measurement. It should not be treated as a universal constant.

== Reference-dependent value representation ==
Loss aversion belongs in computational choice modeling as a '''hypothesis about how outcome value depends on a reference point'''. In a reference-dependent representation, an outcome can be coded as a change relative to a baseline rather than solely as final wealth or another absolute state. A model may assign a larger negative value to a specified loss than the positive value assigned to an equal-sized gain.

The relevant technical objects are the '''reference state, outcome representation, preference or value function, and observed choices'''. The size of any asymmetry has to be estimated for a task and population. A universal loss-aversion coefficient must not be assumed, and an apparently loss-averse decision may also reflect information, risk, endowment effects or transaction costs.

For [[Related to::Decision theory|decision models]] and [[Related to::Prospect theory|prospect theory]], the useful question is whether a reference-dependent model predicts the observed choices better than a specified alternative. Descriptive results about people do not establish that a digital optimizer or agent possesses human feelings about losses.

